  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14094872/assets/6e30e1a16566e13a418ac43569c3eba69e752075232a5458d39d8ac9bd9c1c22.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}=-5x+2xy \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}=11y-3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t),y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n$\\,$\nThe trajectory \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}=-ax+bxy \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}=cy-dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements:\n\n- The trajectory $\\text{circles around}$ $\\text{the nonzero equilibrium}.$\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey $\\text{increases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{increases}.$",
